An optimal observation for this problem is:
1. Leading `'1'`s can never be changed to anything better, so they remain `'1'`.
2. Operation 2 (`"10" -> "01"`) allows us to move any `'0'` to the left past any `'1'`s, meaning all `'0'`s that appear after the first `'0'` can be grouped together with the first `'0'` into a contiguous block.
3. Operation 1 (`"00" -> "10"`) allows a block of $k$ zeros (`"00...0"`) to be transformed into $k-1$ ones followed by a single zero (`"11...10"`).

Thus, if the string has no `'0'`s or only one `'0'`, it is already maximal. Otherwise, if there are `zeros` zeros and the first zero is at index `firstZero`, the resulting string will have `'1'` everywhere except at index `firstZero + zeros - 1`, which will be `'0'`.

### Java Solution

```java
class Solution {
    public String maximumBinaryString(String binary) {
        int n = binary.length();
        int firstZero = binary.indexOf('0');

        // If there are no zeros or only one zero, no improvement can be made
        if (firstZero == -1) {
            return binary;
        }

        int zeros = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (binary.charAt(i) == '0') {
                zeros++;
            }
        }

        if (zeros <= 1) {
            return binary;
        }

        char[] result = new char[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(result, '1');
        result[firstZero + zeros - 1] = '0';

        return new String(result);
    }
}
