An optimal and elegant solution in Java using the sliding window (two pointers) technique.

### Method Explanation

To harvest all fruits in an interval $[L, R]$, the minimum number of steps required starting from `startPos` is:
$$\text{steps} = (R - L) + \min(|startPos - L|, |startPos - R|)$$

This formula works universally:
- If both $L$ and $R$ are to the left of `startPos` ($R \le startPos$), the required steps simplify to $startPos - L$.
- If both are to the right ($L \ge startPos$), it simplifies to $R - startPos$.
- If $L \le startPos \le R$, it represents going to one end first and then turning around to the other end.

Since the steps required to cover $[L, R]$ monotonically increase as the interval widens, we can maintain a sliding window `[left, right]`. For each fruit index `right`, we add its fruit amount to our running sum and then advance `left` as long as the steps required to cover $[fruits[left][0], fruits[right][0]]$ exceed $k$. We then update the maximum fruits collected.

### Java Source Code

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxTotalFruits(int[][] fruits, int startPos, int k) {
        int n = fruits.length;
        int maxFruits = 0;
        int currentSum = 0;
        int left = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
            currentSum += fruits[right][1];

            while (left <= right && getMinSteps(fruits[left][0], fruits[right][0], startPos) > k) {
                currentSum -= fruits[left][1];
                left++;
            }

            maxFruits = Math.max(maxFruits, currentSum);
        }

        return maxFruits;
    }

    private int getMinSteps(int leftPos, int rightPos, int startPos) {
        return (rightPos - leftPos) + Math.min(Math.abs(startPos - leftPos), Math.abs(startPos - rightPos));
    }
}
